WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:02.400
Instructor: Okay, so now that we have installed Git,

00:02.400 --> 00:04.119
whether you're on a Mac or a Windows machine,

00:04.119 --> 00:05.880
the next thing we need to do is

00:05.880 --> 00:08.040
configure our name and email.

00:08.040 --> 00:10.110
So we need to tell Git who we are

00:10.110 --> 00:14.160
who it should attribute work to on our machine.

00:14.160 --> 00:16.350
So this is not tied to an account necessarily.

00:16.350 --> 00:17.247
There's no password.

00:17.247 --> 00:19.140
You can make this whatever you want

00:19.140 --> 00:21.583
but let me just demonstrate the significance

00:21.583 --> 00:24.480
of setting up our name.

00:24.480 --> 00:26.100
So if I'm working on a project with a bunch

00:26.100 --> 00:29.318
of people, for example, this is a project I have, I'm

00:29.318 --> 00:32.280
I really haven't done anything sort of faking it.

00:32.280 --> 00:35.817
It's--it's React, which is a very large JavaScript library.

00:35.817 --> 00:38.528
I'm gonna just run Quick command here

00:38.528 --> 00:41.880
and what you're looking at right now, full screen this

00:41.880 --> 00:44.223
is a log of different work.

00:44.223 --> 00:48.630
Different checkpoints on this application and who did them.

00:48.630 --> 00:52.320
So here's one made by me, Colt Steele.

00:52.320 --> 00:54.090
But I can go back and see, you know

00:54.090 --> 00:56.663
here's some other work done by someone named Andrew Clark

00:56.663 --> 00:58.650
and somebody else did some other work.

00:58.650 --> 00:59.751
Here's more Andrew Clark.

00:59.751 --> 01:01.342
Here's Brian Vaughn.

01:01.342 --> 01:03.540
So the point here is that, oh

01:03.540 --> 01:05.940
here's Dan Abramov, pretty famous developer.

01:05.940 --> 01:10.200
Anyway, the idea is that Git knows who did what work

01:10.200 --> 01:12.570
and who is making these checkpoints and these changes.

01:12.570 --> 01:14.460
So we need to configure that.

01:14.460 --> 01:17.100
So this does not have to match some account

01:17.100 --> 01:18.450
that you have somewhere else.

01:18.450 --> 01:19.309
However, I recommend

01:19.309 --> 01:21.930
that you use the name that you want to be known

01:21.930 --> 01:22.950
by as a developer.

01:22.950 --> 01:24.300
If you are going to publish your work

01:24.300 --> 01:25.270
and put it up on GitHub.

01:25.270 --> 01:28.050
You want to use a name that you know people know you by

01:28.050 --> 01:30.030
or that will be consistent.

01:30.030 --> 01:31.487
You don't wanna be changing this all the time.

01:31.487 --> 01:33.864
And then your email, I recommend configuring

01:33.864 --> 01:37.020
with the email that you'll set up for your GitHub account

01:37.020 --> 01:38.113
if you haven't done so already.

01:38.113 --> 01:40.590
Later in the course when we talk about GitHub

01:40.590 --> 01:42.930
we can cross that bridge and you can always

01:42.930 --> 01:44.790
reconfigure these two values.

01:44.790 --> 01:46.105
So here's how we do it.

01:46.105 --> 01:48.600
The first step, we'll do our name.

01:48.600 --> 01:52.830
So all we do is Git config and then dash dash Global.

01:52.830 --> 01:55.097
That's a way of telling Git to set our username

01:55.097 --> 01:59.550
or our user.name to be the same name across every time

01:59.550 --> 02:00.383
we use Git.

02:00.383 --> 02:02.460
Every instance, but you can always change this.

02:02.460 --> 02:06.075
So Git config dash dash global user.name, and then in quotes

02:06.075 --> 02:08.161
the name you want Git to know you by.

02:08.161 --> 02:10.200
So I can try this right now.

02:10.200 --> 02:11.220
I'm not gonna configure it.

02:11.220 --> 02:13.931
I want to show you, I can actually just check.

02:13.931 --> 02:17.250
Do you have a user.name for me already Git?

02:17.250 --> 02:19.416
If I run this command, I'm not setting it, I'm asking

02:19.416 --> 02:22.710
and it says, yep, you're Colt Steele.

02:22.710 --> 02:27.710
However, on my Windows side, if I do Git config user.name.

02:29.370 --> 02:30.480
I haven't configured one.

02:30.480 --> 02:32.100
So it shows me nothing.

02:32.100 --> 02:33.182
So I'm gonna set that now.

02:33.182 --> 02:37.620
So Git config dash dash global user.name and then

02:37.620 --> 02:39.240
in quotes, the name I want.

02:39.240 --> 02:43.297
So Git config dash dash global user.name

02:43.297 --> 02:46.020
and I'll do Colt Steele.

02:46.020 --> 02:47.880
Obviously you should put your name there.

02:47.880 --> 02:49.230
I'm gonna hit enter.

02:49.230 --> 02:51.810
We don't really see anything happen, but now if I try

02:51.810 --> 02:53.760
and retrieve it, that's what this line does.

02:53.760 --> 02:56.689
Git what is user.name?

02:56.689 --> 02:59.940
It tells me Colt Steele and we're good to go.

02:59.940 --> 03:02.310
Now we're gonna do the same thing for email.

03:02.310 --> 03:03.570
So it's the exact same pattern

03:03.570 --> 03:08.104
except user.email and then provide your email address.

03:08.104 --> 03:09.600
So we'll try this.

03:09.600 --> 03:10.777
It's the same process.

03:10.777 --> 03:13.230
I'll do this on the the Windows side

03:13.230 --> 03:15.720
but whatever platform you're on, you want to do this

03:15.720 --> 03:18.266
we'll do Git config user.email.

03:18.266 --> 03:20.283
Nothing is there.

03:20.283 --> 03:25.283
But now I'm gonna say Git config dash dash global user.email

03:26.040 --> 03:26.924
and then set it to be

03:26.924 --> 03:31.440
this is not my real email, but colt@gmail.co.

03:31.440 --> 03:34.350
Again, not my real email, but I recommend you put a

03:34.350 --> 03:35.391
an email you have access to

03:35.391 --> 03:38.517
and especially when we sign up for a GitHub account.

03:38.517 --> 03:41.782
If you can foresee the email you would use for GitHub

03:41.782 --> 03:44.133
I would use that email now.

03:44.133 --> 03:45.750
But we can always change it.

03:45.750 --> 03:48.060
It's the exact same line to change it.

03:48.060 --> 03:50.684
Okay, so now I can recall the line from earlier.

03:50.684 --> 03:53.730
If you don't know how to do this, hit the up arrow.

03:53.730 --> 03:55.772
I can scroll through different lines up and down

03:55.772 --> 03:57.603
and I'm just gonna verify

03:57.603 --> 04:00.540
did it set and it did.

04:00.540 --> 04:02.490
Okay. So that's all we needed to do.

04:02.490 --> 04:06.120
Those two lines are going to tailgate who we are so that

04:06.120 --> 04:09.035
for future work, when we make changes, they are attributed

04:09.035 --> 04:12.841
to whatever that user.name and user.email are set to.

04:12.841 --> 04:13.803
Alright.
